+## 23. Mahabharata dialogues, witnesses and aftermath
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-004-146 | How does the Sulabha-Janaka dialogue examine identity and authority? | Read the complete bounded dialogue in a specified translation and commentary; keep philosophical terms contextual and avoid reducing the exchange to a modern slogan. |
+| BANK-004-147 | What choices does Kunti describe when Karna learns about his birth? | Follow the encounter's timing, requests and responses in one edition; label later dramatizations and preserve the distinction between narrated knowledge and audience knowledge. |
+| BANK-004-148 | How does Gandhari's grief shape the Mahabharata's account of victory? | Trace selected postwar passages and their speakers; avoid editing a lament into an uncomplicated endorsement of any side. |
+| BANK-004-149 | What does the mongoose episode question about the meaning of giving? | Read the episode within its narrative setting and compare one attributed interpretation; distinguish its ethical contrast from a prescription for household donations. |
+| BANK-004-150 | How do accounts of Abhimanyu's death distribute knowledge and responsibility among characters? | Use a specified edition to track who knows the formation, actions and reported events; exclude unsupported claims about prenatal learning as established science. |
+
+## 24. Regional-language devotional canons
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
